      "text": "Since the absolute value of a number is non-negative, we have two cases to consider:\n\nCase 1: $n + 6 \\ge 0$, which means $n \\ge -6$. In this case, the equation simplifies to:\n\\[\nn + 6 = 2 - n.\n\\]\nAdding $n$ to both sides yields:\n\\[\n2n + 6 = 2.\n\\]\nSubtracting 6 from both sides gives:\n\\[\n2n = -4.\n\\]\nDividing both sides by 2, we find:\n\\[\nn = -2.\n\\]\nWe must verify if this solution satisfies the original equation. Substituting $n = -2$ into the original equation, we get:\n\\[\n|-2 + 6| = |4| = 4,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n2 - (-2) = 2 + 2 = 4.\n\\]\nSince both sides are equal, $n = -2$ is a valid solution for this case.\n\nCase 2: $n + 6 < 0$, which means $n < -6$. In this case, the equation becomes:\n\\[\n-(n + 6) = 2 - n.\n\\]\nSimplifying, we get:\n\\[\n-n - 6 = 2 - n.\n\\]\nAdding $n$ to both sides gives:\n\\[\n-6 = 2.\n\\]\nThis is a contradiction, meaning there are no solutions in this case because an absolute value cannot be equal to a negative number.\n\nTherefore, the only solution for $n$ is $\\boxed{n = -2}$.\n\nLet's verify this solution computationally to ensure accuracy.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nn = sp.symbols('n')\n\n# Define the equation\nequation = sp.Abs(n + 6) - (2 - n)\n\n# Solve the equation\nsolution = sp.solve(equation, n)\nprint(solution)\n```",
