      "text": "To solve the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\), we need to consider the definition of the absolute value function. The absolute value equation \\( |x| = y \\) can be broken down into two separate cases:\n\n1. \\( x = y \\)\n2. \\( x = -y \\)\n\nIn our case, \\( x = n + 6 \\) and \\( y = 2 - n \\). Therefore, we have two cases to consider:\n\n### Case 1: \\( n + 6 = 2 - n \\)\n\n1. Start by isolating \\( n \\) on one side of the equation:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = 2 - n\n   \\]\n2. Add \\( n \\) to both sides to combine like terms:\n   \\[\n   n + n + 6 = 2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2n + 6 = 2\n   \\]\n3. Subtract 6 from both sides:\n   \\[\n   2n = 2 - 6\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2n = -4\n   \\]\n4. Divide both sides by 2:\n   \\[\n   n = \\frac{-4}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n = -2\n   \\]\n\n### Case 2: \\( n + 6 = -(2 - n) \\)\n\n1. Start by simplifying the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 = -2 + n\n   \\]\n2. Subtract \\( n \\) from both sides to isolate the constant term:\n   \\[\n   n + 6 - n = -2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   6 = -2\n   \\]\n\nThis is a contradiction, which means there is no solution for the second case.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe only solution to the equation \\( |n + 6| = 2 - n \\) is from Case 1, where \\( n = -2 \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2}\n\\]",
